$$\\begin{cases} 3x + 2y = 19 \\\\ x - y = 3 \\end{cases}$$
What is the value of $y$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '2', ARRAY['2'], E'From the second equation, $x = y + 3$. Substitute into the first equation: $3(y + 3) + 2y = 19$. Distribute to get $3y + 9 + 2y = 19$, which simplifies to $5y + 9 = 19$. Subtract $9$ from both sides: $5y = 10$. Divide by $5$: $y = 2$. To verify, $x = 2 + 3 = 5$, and $3(5) + 2(2) = 15 + 4 = 19$. A common error is solving for $x$ instead of $y$, which gives $5$. Another error is distributing incorrectly in the substitution step, such as writing $3y + 3$ instead of $3y + 9$.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







