If $g(a) = 0$, what is the value of $a$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$\\frac{7}{2}$"},{"key":"B","text":"$3$"},{"key":"C","text":"$\\frac{11}{2}$"},{"key":"D","text":"$\\frac{5}{2}$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Using the points $(1, 5)$ and $(4, -1)$, the slope is $\\frac{-1 - 5}{4 - 1} = \\frac{-6}{3} = -2$. The function is $g(x) = -2(x - 1) + 5 = -2x + 7$. Setting $g(a) = 0$: $-2a + 7 = 0$, so $a = \\frac{7}{2}$. The value $3$ results from an arithmetic error when dividing $7$ by $2$ and rounding to the nearest integer. The value $\\frac{11}{2}$ comes from taking the $y$-value $11$ from the table and dividing by the slope magnitude $2$, confusing a table entry with the function''s zero. The value $\\frac{5}{2}$ results from using $g(1) = 5$ as the $y$-intercept directly without computing it, forming $g(x) = -2x + 5$ and solving $-2a + 5 = 0$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);













$$\\begin{cases} x + y = 10 \\\\ x - y = 4 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$3$"},{"key":"B","text":"$5$"},{"key":"C","text":"$7$"},{"key":"D","text":"$14$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Add the two equations: $(x + y) + (x - y) = 10 + 4$, which gives $2x = 14$, so $x = 7$. The value $14$ is the result of finding $2x$ but not dividing by $2$, an incomplete final step. The value $3$ is the value of $y$ (found by substituting $x = 7$ into $x + y = 10$), not the requested value of $x$. The value $5$ results from incorrectly subtracting the equations instead of adding them and then mishandling the arithmetic.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"arithmetic_slip"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 45, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




$$\\begin{cases} 3x + ay = 7 \\\\ 6x + 10y = 3 \\end{cases}$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-5$"},{"key":"B","text":"$\\frac{7}{3}$"},{"key":"C","text":"$5$"},{"key":"D","text":"$20$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel but not identical. This occurs when the ratios of the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ are equal but differ from the ratio of the constants: $\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{a}{10} \\neq \\frac{7}{3}$. From $\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{a}{10}$, cross-multiply to get $30 = 6a$, so $a = 5$. Verify: $\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{5}{10} = \\frac{1}{2}$, while $\\frac{7}{3} \\neq \\frac{1}{2}$, confirming the lines are parallel and distinct. The value $-5$ introduces a sign error in the cross-multiplication. The value $\\frac{7}{3}$ confuses the constant ratio with the coefficient ratio needed for parallel lines. The value $20$ results from incorrectly setting $\\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{10}{a}$ and solving for $a$.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"equation_setup_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-16"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-16","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);
























